  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 516,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 555,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70503,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 154,
        "headingId": 555,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Many mixed sex cord stromal tumours are white to yellow and replace most or all of the testis, often growing in proximity to the rete testis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:55:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 510,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 549,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70504,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 154,
        "headingId": 549,
        "headingText": "<p>Mixed sex cord stromal tumour contains (in varying combinations) Leydig, Sertoli, and granulosa cells intermixed with a stromal component with spindled morphology. The sex cord elements are arranged in sheets, small nests, tubules, or trabeculae. Small cysts are present with Call&ndash;Exner&ndash;like bodies. The stromal component generally consists of spindle-shaped cells in a collagenous background intimately admixed with the sex cord element {10757400; 33457255}. Cellular pleomorphism and mitotic figures are variable. Rarely there are admixed germ cells; typically, these are most conspicuous peripherally {10757400}. This phenomenon may raise the consideration of a mixed germ cell sex cord stromal tumour where the germ cells have malignant features {28445692}. Reticulin stain can be helpful in the diagnosis because the sex cord elements have inconspicuous reticulin fibres, whereas stromal cells are individually surrounded by reticulin fibres {28445692}. Tumour cells, single or in aggregates, can show reactivity for inhibin {9237180} and WT1, and they are positive for SMA and S100. Focally they show positivity for SF1, calretinin, CD34, and occasionally EMA. Focal nuclear expression of DMRT1 and/or positivity for SALL4 may raise the possibility of an intermixed germ cell component (observed in mixed germ cell&ndash;sex cord stromal tumour of the testis) {32447491}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2022-04-15T15:57:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 511,
